# This case's config names the neighbor 127.0.0.2, where upstream names
# 127.0.0.1. api-healthcheck-withdraw.conf carries the reason: on the loopback the peer
# address and the speaker's own address collided, and RFC 4271 Section 5.1.3
# then withheld the route. The peer moved; the expected frames did not.
#
# ExaBGP renders `neighbor.address.peer` from the CONFIGURED neighbor address,
# not from the socket: `peer_addr = neighbor.session.peer_address`
# (src/exabgp/reactor/api/response/json.py), and `peer_address` is parsed from
# the `neighbor <address>` token (src/exabgp/configuration/neighbor/__init__.py).
# So the document this case owes carries 127.0.0.2. `local` stays 127.0.0.1,
# and so does every next hop in the routes below.
option=file:api-healthcheck-withdraw.conf
1:cmd:announce eor ipv4 unicast
1:raw:FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF:0017:02:00000000
1:cmd:neighbor * announce route 10.0.0.1/32 next-hop self origin igp local-preference 100
1:raw:FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF:0031:02:00000015400101004002004003047F00000140050400000064200A000001
1:json:{"exabgp":"6.0.0","type":"update","neighbor":{"address":{"local":"127.0.0.1","peer":"127.0.0.2"},"asn":{"local":1,"peer":1},"router-id":"1.2.3.4","direction":"in","message":{"update":{"attribute":{"origin":"igp","local-preference":100},"announce":{"ipv4 unicast":{"127.0.0.1":[{"nlri":"10.0.0.1/32"}]}}}}}}
1:cmd:neighbor * withdraw route 10.0.0.1/32 next-hop self
1:raw:FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF:001C:02:0005200A0000010000
1:json:{"exabgp":"6.0.0","type":"update","neighbor":{"address":{"local":"127.0.0.1","peer":"127.0.0.2"},"asn":{"local":1,"peer":1},"router-id":"1.2.3.4","direction":"in","message":{"update":{"withdraw":{"ipv4 unicast":[{"nlri":"10.0.0.1/32"}]}}}}}
